# bridge-qos1 — the bridge shape: ONE publisher connection per generator carries every
# topic, as a site gateway forwards all of a plant's data over one bridge connection, and
# each topic goes to its own `$share` group of durable consumers: 10 topics (`load/0` ..
# `load/9`, one per data type) x 4 members = 40 subscribers. Durable QoS 1, 200-byte
# payloads, as durable-qos1.
#
# What it measures that the 20,000-client shapes do not: everything one connection can
# carry. Its publishes are read by one connection task and dispatched by the one hub shard
# that owns the client (ADR 0093 D1), whatever node.hub_shards is; its QoS 1 window is the
# broker's Receive Maximum (MQTTD_RECEIVE_MAXIMUM, 256 by default), so at the default its
# rate is at most 256 / the ack latency. A run that is to find the broker's ceiling, not
# the default's, raises it with EXTRA_BROKER_ENV. With so few connections, packets carry
# many messages each, so the per-VM connection limit of a Hetzner host
# (memory: hetzner-packet-rate-ceiling) does not decide the result.
#
# Values here are defaults: anything exported in the environment wins.
QOS=${QOS:-1}
SHAPE=${SHAPE:-share}
SESSIONS=${SESSIONS:-durable}
SESSION_EXPIRY=${SESSION_EXPIRY:-30}
PAYLOAD=${PAYLOAD:-200}
TOPICS=${TOPICS:-10}
CONSUMERS=${CONSUMERS:-4}
SHARE_GROUPS=${SHARE_GROUPS:-1}
# One connection per generator, every topic on it (mqtt-load --bridge).
BRIDGE=${BRIDGE:-1}
PUBLISHERS=${PUBLISHERS:-1}
RATE_PER_PUBLISHER=${RATE_PER_PUBLISHER:-50000}
# The connection's QoS 1 window, capped by the broker's Receive Maximum.
INFLIGHT=${INFLIGHT:-8192}
LATENCY_GATE=${LATENCY_GATE:-ack}
START_RATE_local=${START_RATE_local:-500}
START_RATE_n3=${START_RATE_n3:-20000}
START_RATE_n10=${START_RATE_n10:-20000}
START_RATE_h3=${START_RATE_h3:-20000}
